-A separate article category would gain support from a cluster of synchronic restrictions shared by \mention{the}, \mention{a} and \mention{every}, absent elsewhere in Noun and otherwise requiring repeated exceptions. Lack of independent argument use alone doesn't supply that cluster.
+The D-noun analysis retains the shared determining profile and records the absence of independent use in lexical permissions. The articles' nounhood depends on their membership in that broader system. They don't individually exhibit the full evidence that supports the parent category.
+
+\subsection{The pronoun boundary: \mentionhead{who}, \mentionhead{which}, and \mentionhead{what}}\label{sec:wh-boundary}
+
+\mention{Who} has positive morphological grounds for pronoun status: its paradigm includes nominative \mention{who}, accusative \mention{whom}, and genitive \mention{whose} \citep[904--905]{huddleston2002}. \mention{What} lacks a comparable case paradigm. That absence alone wouldn't exclude it from pronoun, but independent use and a non-personal interpretation don't establish pronounhood either. Both properties occur among determinatives, including the \mention{-thing} compounds.
+
+\textit{CGEL} assigns interrogative \mention{what} to two categories. Before an overt nominal it's determinative, as in \mention{what book} or \mention{what person}; independently it's a non-personal pronoun, as in \mention{What arrived?} Relative \mention{what}/\mention{whatever} receives a corresponding split. The stated contrast concerns gender: the independent forms are non-personal, while the determinatives permit personal and non-personal heads \citep[398]{huddleston2002}.
+
+I treat these occurrences as determinatives throughout. With an overt nominal, that nominal supplies the restriction; without one, the construction requires a non-personal interpretation. This states the contrast as a use condition instead of assigning a second lexical category on its basis. The determinative analysis preserves \mention{what}'s connection across the constructions while distinguishing its gender restrictions from those of \mention{which}.
+
+Interrogative \mention{which} is already determinative in \textit{CGEL} both before a nominal and independently: compare \mention{Which candidate arrived?} and \mention{Which arrived?} With candidates under discussion, the latter can be personal. It also permits \mention{which of the candidates}, whereas \ungram{\mention{what of the candidates}} doesn't have the corresponding selective reading \citep[397--398]{huddleston2002}. Determinative membership therefore doesn't predict identical gender or partitive permissions.
+
+Relative \mention{which} poses the same boundary question as \mention{what}. \textit{CGEL} treats it as determinative in \mention{at which stage}, but as a non-personal pronoun in \mention{the post office, which is nearby} \citep[399]{huddleston2002}. I retain determinative here too, with the non-personal restriction attached to this relative construction. This changes lexical classification, leaving relative-clause syntax, including fused relatives, to be analysed separately. Either classification places these words within the proposed superordinate Noun.
+
+Exclamative \mention{what} in \mention{What a nice day!} remains a separate case. Following \textit{CGEL}, I retain its adjective analysis here (§\ref{sec:membership}). A common spelling doesn't require one lexeme across all constructions. Nor does being a \term{pro-form} determine lexical category: gender-sensitive interpretation must be distinguished from the grammatical evidence for pronoun membership \citep{reynolds2025proformgender}.
+
+\subsection{Form selection and degree modification}\label{sec:enough}
+
+\mention{No}/\mention{none} illustrates a restriction on forms within a paradigm. \textit{CGEL} treats them as inflectional forms of one determinative: dependent \mention{no students} contrasts with independent \mention{none}, while both permit \mention{almost} \citep[389--390]{huddleston2002}. The D-noun analysis preserves this relation. Ordinary Head structure licenses \mention{none of the students}; it doesn't license \ungram{\mention{no of the students}}. Form selection remains necessary, just as with \mention{my}/\mention{mine}.
+
+\mention{Enough} tests both position and external function. It precedes a nominal in \mention{enough money} and can follow one in \mention{money enough}; post-head \mention{enough} can't itself be premodified, as shown by \ungram{\mention{money almost enough}} \citep[396--397, 445]{huddleston2002}. Only the permitted construction licenses each position.
+
+\mention{Enough} also modifies adjectives, adverbs, verbs, and some PPs: \mention{good enough}, \mention{quickly enough}, \mention{I hadn't prepared enough}, and \mention{enough in control} \citep[396--397]{huddleston2002}. The degree determinatives \mention{much} and \mention{little}, and \mention{no}/\mention{none}, likewise have uses outside NP structure \citep[390, 395--397]{huddleston2002}. These permissions must survive reclassification. Noun membership doesn't confine every projection to argument or determiner function.
+
+NPs already modify adjectives in \mention{three years old}, \mention{a great deal smaller}, and \mention{plenty big enough} \citep[549--550]{huddleston2002}. These include the quantificational common nouns compared in §\ref{sec:membership}. Degree modification therefore supplies another connection with existing nouns, but also another restriction: \textit{CGEL} contrasts predicative \mention{a great deal better} with its exclusion before a noun in \ungram{\mention{some a great deal better proposals}}, where \mention{much better} is permitted \citep[551--552]{huddleston2002}.
+
+The D-noun analysis must preserve that difference between two kinds of NP modifier. It expands NP's range of degree-modifier uses while restricting their positions by head and construction. The resulting cost is that the existing ban on pre-head NP modifiers within attributive AdjPs becomes a narrower condition. Nominal projection alone doesn't license \mention{enough}'s full distribution.
 
